Premature Rupture of Membranes
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Sudden gush of vaginal fluid, Pooling in posterior fornix, Positive ferning test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-041
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a large gush of clear vaginal fluid at 35 weeks gestation, raising concern for preterm premature rupture of membranes.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a sudden, continuous leakage of clear fluid from the vagina in a preterm pregnancy, causing acute anxiety regarding imminent delivery and infection risk. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately confirm membrane rupture, assess for cord prolapse, evaluate fetal well-being, and coordinate ambulance transport to a maternity unit before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am 35 weeks pregnant, and water just gushed out of me when I stood up, and it is still trickling down my legs.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden gush of clear fluid from the vagina 45 minutes ago, followed by continuous slow leakage. This occurred before any onset of labor pains. She is currently at 35 weeks gestation. She notes that coughing (Valsalva maneuver) or standing exacerbates the fluid leakage. She explicitly denies any regular uterine contractions, severe abdominal pain, or vaginal bleeding. She reports normal fetal movements prior to the fluid loss. She feels well and denies any fever or chills.
Functional Status: Resting on the couch; afraid to move due to continuous fluid loss.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of chorioamnionitis and umbilical cord prolapse; partner is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious but is in no acute physical distress. Her lower garments are visibly soaked with clear fluid.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a soft, non-tender uterus; no clinical signs of chorioamnionitis (uterine tenderness) or active contractions. Fetal heart rate via handheld Doppler is 145 bpm, regular, with no decelerations noted. Sterile speculum examination (no digital exam performed) demonstrates clear fluid tracking from the cervical os and obvious pooling of amniotic fluid in the posterior vaginal fornix. A point-of-care Nitrazine test of the vaginal fluid demonstrates a distinct alkaline pH shift (turns blue), and a sample dried on a slide shows a positive ferning test under portable microscopy (if available, otherwise clinical signs are sufficient for transfer).
Environmental Observations: Soaked towels on the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking prenatal vitamins.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Preterm Premature Rupture of Membranes (PPROM) - Status: Confirmed, stable
Differentials:

Severe Urinary Incontinence - Ruled out by the positive Nitrazine test confirming the alkaline nature of the fluid, and the continuous pooling in the vaginal vault.

Profuse Physiologic Leukorrhea - Unlikely to present as a sudden massive gush of thin, clear fluid that soaks clothing.

Cord Prolapse - Safely ruled out by the direct visualization of the cervix via sterile speculum showing no cord, and the normal fetal heart rate.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Medical risk requires hospital admission)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic diagnostic tetrad of premature rupture of membranes: a sudden gush of fluid, pooling in the posterior fornix on sterile speculum exam, and a positive chemical (Nitrazine/ferning) test, occurring without active labor. The objective confirmation of normal fetal heart tones and the absence of a prolapsed cord are reassuring. However, the confirmed rupture at 35 weeks carries a high risk of ascending infection (chorioamnionitis) and impending preterm labor. Urgent after-hours intervention was necessary to clinically confirm the diagnosis without performing a dangerous digital exam, and to arrange immediate transport to the obstetric hospital for prophylactic antibiotics and fetal monitoring.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed sterile speculum examination to confirm diagnosis and rule out cord prolapse; strictly avoided digital vaginal examination. Auscultated fetal heart tones. Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for routine transport to the local maternity hospital.
Positive Behavioral Change: To completely minimize the risk of a dangerous infection entering the womb, absolutely nothing must be inserted into the vagina (no tampons, no intercourse); understanding that the protective sac is broken will ensure strict compliance. To assist the hospital staff, wear a clean maternity pad during the transport so they can assess the color and volume of the ongoing fluid loss; recognizing the value of this visual evidence will ensure you do not discard it.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on route.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is awaiting ambulance transport. Inform paramedics immediately if you feel a sudden urge to push, if you feel something physically drop into the vagina, or if the fluid turns green or bloody.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The protective sac around the baby has broken; hospital admission is required to monitor for infection and prepare for potential early delivery.
Clinical Course: The fluid will continue to leak, as the baby continues to produce it. You will be monitored closely at the hospital and likely given antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk around. Keep a pad on and wait for the paramedics. Tell them immediately if you feel contractions starting.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bag of waters that protects your baby has ruptured prematurely. Because you are at 35 weeks, the baby is nearly full term, but without that protective bag, there is a high risk of bacteria travelling up and causing an infection. It is absolutely critical that no internal examinations are done right now. The ambulance will take you to the maternity ward where they will monitor the baby's heart rate continuously and give you antibiotics to prevent infection.
Actionable Steps: Remain resting on the couch. Wear a thick maternity pad. Do not use the toilet to bear down or push.

